-- title:
--   Marked arcs are exactly the paired increments
-- statement:
--   Let $G$ be an abelian group. Let $a,b,h\in G$ satisfy $2h=0$, $h\ne0$, $a\ne b$, and $a\ne b+h$. Set $U=\{a,b,b+h\}$. Then
--
--   $$\bigl(x\to y\text{ and }x+h\to y\bigr)\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad y=x+b\text{ or }y=x+b+h.$$
--
--   Arcs use increments in $U$. This recovers the paired increments as the marked color.
-- source:
--   Alexandre Couret, https://github.com/alexcour/couret-finite-verifications/blob/65b828d9ffd016ce2a1b05403f303fa5f948c10f/research/correspondants_20261013/lean/CayleyDoubletCore.lean#L398-L433, lines 398–433; source file SHA-256 7162f949e4d7aece3f9241308253678a1dadd2e7255ff303aab69f6bf9731a4a; theorem Couret.CayleyDoublet.markedArc_iff_pair

